    "summary": "Mellow island set glows with stellar Mason’s Children and crisp blues",
    "blurb": "The single‑set show delivers a concise, island‑friendly flow highlighted by a clean Mason’s Children and a standout Cumberland Blues. Jerry’s phrasing on Black Peter and a relaxed Me & My Uncle add extra charm, while Pigpen caps the night with a strong Good Lovin’ encore.",
    "review": "On January 24, 1970 the Dead took the stage at Honolulu Civic Auditorium for a brief but memorable single‑set performance that perfectly matched the laid‑back island vibe. The band opened with a tight‑pacing run through “Cumberland Blues” and kept the momentum rolling into “Cold Rain And Snow,” “Me And My Uncle,” and a soulful rendition of “I’m A King Bee.” The highlight came with an immaculate “Mason’s Children,” captured in crystal‑clear SBD quality, followed by a beautifully phrased “Black Peter” that showcases Jerry Garcia’s nuanced guitar work – arguably his best Hawaiian playing. Pigpen’s presence shone on the closing “Good Lovin’” encore, delivering a gritty, energetic finish. The recording, sourced from the Kaplan soundboard tape, is praised for its superb mix and minimal drop‑outs, making it the definitive document of this short but sweet show.",
